                        "entry_id": "a5295998-bdba-3ee5-b790-a6241df18935",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The knowledge, acquired through meditation, that one's self (atman) is identical with Ultimate Reality (Brahman)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Hinduism",
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fd693b67-53fb-4adc-a6cd-47e138e27d49",
                                "sense_id": "a2caf9a3-ebe9-33fd-aae0-ca01e0f3aa41",
                                "text": "1986, The Vedanta Kesari, Ramakrishna Math, page 316,\nThe knower of Atman realizes Him by Jnana Yoga (the path of knowledge)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "97290cf7-58bc-4326-a7e8-97da71e68334",
                                "sense_id": "a2caf9a3-ebe9-33fd-aae0-ca01e0f3aa41",
                                "text": "1969, Swami Rāmānandasarasvatī, The Hindu Ideal, Sri Ramananda Centenary Memorial Committee, page 291,\nThe dualists, Sri Vaishnavas in particular, seem to entertain a strong opinion that Bhakti alone is the cause of Moksha, and that Jnana is but a step towards Bhakti."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cb7d8912-7109-4c12-9cc7-9eb6070ce1fb",
                                "sense_id": "a2caf9a3-ebe9-33fd-aae0-ca01e0f3aa41",
                                "text": "Jnana is a direct knowing of things as they really are."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "cdb1db78-617d-3e05-90da-87c96559a107",
                        "entry_id": "a5295998-bdba-3ee5-b790-a6241df18935",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Pure awareness that is free of conceptual encumbrances."
                        ],
